  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1248 PM CST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nSurface high pressure has settled into the area this afternoon \nas a large trough pivots across the Great Lakes region. While \nlow clouds have eroded early this afternoon, high clouds are \nbeginning to overspread the region. Temperatures remain cool in\nthe upper 30s and low 40s this afternoon. Despite increasing\ncloud cover, it will be chilly tonight as temperatures fall into\nthe upper teens and low 20s. Tuesday sees similar conditions to\nMonday, though skies remain mostly cloudy.\n\nHeading into Wednesday, a chance of flurries enters the\nforecast. A trough over the northern Plains will begin to sag\nsouth as it phases with another trough over the southern Plains.\nThe northern trough begins to cut-off from the main flow over \nthe central Plains during this time. With limited moist\nadvection, precipitation chances remain low, but enough ascent\nunder the developing upper-low is expected to generate flurries\nover northeast Nebraska and western Iowa. No impacts are\nexpected as amounts will be very light and very dry air near the\nsurface greatly limits what actually reaches the ground. \n\nBeyond this passing weather system, temperatures remain cool in\nthe upper 30s and low 40s through Thursday before a warming\ntrend begins as we head towards the weekend. Dry conditions\nremain the main story outside of the aforementioned flurries.\n\nFriday through the weekend sees temperatures warm back through\nthe 40s into the 50s. Our next weather system approaches the\nregion on Saturday. Current guidance only forecasts a glancing\nblow from this system, so rain chances remain low. Warmer\nweather appears likely as we transition into next week.",
